<article class="env-lemma" id="015C">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/015C">Lemma <span data-tag="015C">13.16.4</span>.</a> Let $F : \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{B}$ be an additive functor between abelian categories and assume $RF : D^{+}(\mathcal{A}) \to D^{+}(\mathcal{B})$ is everywhere defined. Let $A$ be an object of $\mathcal{A}$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$A$ is right acyclic for $F$ if and only if $F(A) \to R^0F(A)$ is an isomorphism and $R^ iF(A) = 0$ for all $i > 0$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if $F$ is left exact, then $A$ is right acyclic for $F$ if and only if $R^ iF(A) = 0$ for all $i > 0$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $A$ is right acyclic for $F$, then $RF(A[0]) = F(A)[0]$ and in particular $F(A) \to R^0F(A)$ is an isomorphism and $R^ iF(A) = 0$ for $i \not= 0$. Conversely, if $F(A) \to R^0F(A)$ is an isomorphism and $R^ iF(A) = 0$ for all $i > 0$ then $F(A[0]) \to RF(A[0])$ is a quasi-isomorphism by Lemma <a href="/tag/05TD" data-tag="05TD">13.16.3</a> part (1) and hence $A$ is acyclic. If $F$ is left exact then $F = R^0F$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/05TD" data-tag="05TD">13.16.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>